| A linked file's handlers see that file, and it survives the link.
|
| `count` is declared in the module's top level, which is over by the time
| any of this runs — but its handlers still change it and still agree about
| it. That is what makes a module able to hold anything at all: the world its
| handlers were written in outlives the statements that built it.
link "modules/isolated_world.code"

| What it exported travelled up. What it kept did not: `count` is not a name
| here, which `fail_link_private_flatten.code` holds from the other side.
assert started = "yes"

emit Bump { by = 2 } to this get first
assert first.total = 2

| A second handler in the same file, sharing the one world.
emit Bump { by = 5 } to this get second
assert second.total = 7

emit Total {} to this get seen
assert seen.total = 7
